nvproxy: per-sandbox GPU memory limit

What: a runsc flag, --nvproxy-gpu-memory-limit=<bytes>, limits the device
memory that a sandbox's applications may allocate through nvproxy on each of
its GPUs. The flag is on runsc's annotation allowlist, so a container can set
it with the annotation dev.gvisor.flag.nvproxy-gpu-memory-limit, which may set
a limit or lower one set on the runtime, but not raise or remove it. Without
a limit, from the flag or the annotation, nvproxy charges nothing and behaves
as before except where the driver reuses a handle that nvproxy still tracks
(nvproxy logs "handle ... already in use"): nvproxy then unlinks the old
object from the objects it depended on, so that freeing one of them no longer
frees the old object again and untracks the new one under its handle. The old
object, and what depended on it, is then never released. Releasing an object
does nothing without a limit, except an OS descriptor's
(NV01_MEMORY_SYSTEM_OS_DESCRIPTOR), so such a descriptor keeps the application
memory it pinned pinned and mapped in the Sentry.

nvproxy charges each allocation of device memory (NV01_MEMORY_LOCAL_USER, by
NV_ESC_RM_ALLOC or NV_ESC_RM_VID_HEAP_CONTROL) to its GPU, by device instance,
and keeps the sandbox's total on each GPU within the limit:
- Before calling the driver, with the client locked, it charges the requested
  size rounded up to 64 KiB, and fails the allocation with NV_ERR_NO_MEMORY
  (in the parameters, errno 0), as the driver fails an allocation when the GPU
  is full, if that exceeds the limit or nvproxy does not know the GPU. It
  knows the GPU only of a device, or its subdevice, that the client allocated
  itself, not of one it duplicated or imported.
- The driver rounds the size up to the allocation's page size (64 KiB, 2 MiB
  or 512 MiB; mem_mgr_gm107.c:memmgrAllocDetermineAlignment_GM107()) and
  returns it (video_mem.c:vidmemConstruct_IMPL()), and PMA takes 4 KiB pages
  as 64 KiB ones (video_mem.c:_vidmemPmaAllocate()), so the memory taken is
  the returned size rounded up to 64 KiB. nvproxy charges that; when it would
  exceed the limit, nvproxy frees the new object with NV_ESC_RM_FREE, as the
  application's client, and fails the allocation instead.
- It fails NVOS32_ALLOC_FLAGS_FIXED_ADDRESS_ALLOCATE with NV_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED,
  since the driver then takes the size rounded up to the requested alignment,
  which can be far more than the size it returns (_vidmemPmaAllocate()).
The total thus never exceeds the limit when an allocation returns; only if the
driver fails that free does the memory stay allocated and charged beyond it.

A charge lasts while anything nvproxy tracks keeps the memory allocated: a
handle (the one allocated, a duplicate from NV_ESC_RM_DUP_OBJECT, or an import
by NV0000_CTRL_CMD_OS_UNIX_IMPORT_OBJECT(S)_FROM_FD); an export by
NV0000_CTRL_CMD_OS_UNIX_EXPORT_OBJECT(S)_TO_FD, until its file is closed or
its slot is overwritten, as the driver keeps a duplicate in its export client
(rmobjexportimport.c:RmExportObject(), osapi.c:rm_cleanup_file_private()); an
object that the driver makes with duplicates of the memory, which are an
NV_MEMORY_FABRIC object made from it (map.hVidMem;
mem_fabric.c:memoryfabricConstruct_IMPL()), an NV_SEMAPHORE_SURFACE on it
(sem_surf.c:_semsurfDupMemory()) and an NV_MEMORY_MAPPER on such a surface
(mem_mapper.c:memmapperConstruct_IMPL()); and a UVM external mapping
(UVM_MAP_EXTERNAL_ALLOCATION), which UVM backs with a duplicate per GPU
(uvm_map_external.c:uvm_map_external_allocation_on_gpu()). nvproxy keeps the
client locked while UVM maps the memory, so that the handle cannot be freed
and reused meanwhile, and mirrors how UVM splits and removes these mappings: a
later mapping or UVM_UNMAP_EXTERNAL on the same GPU removes the part it
covers, UVM_FREE of the external range, UVM_UNREGISTER_GPU_VASPACE or
UVM_UNREGISTER_GPU of the GPU, and the destruction of the VA space remove the
rest. Handles are released by NV_ESC_RM_FREE, with a freed parent, or in the
teardown of their client when its file is closed; when the driver reuses a
handle nvproxy still tracks, the object it named and the objects that
depended on it release their charges. Where the driver keeps one file's
objects alive for another file, nvproxy keeps the first file's host file open
as long: a file registered with NV_ESC_REGISTER_FD holds the file of its
ctl_fd, with its clients and exports (escape.c:RmIoctl(),
osapi.c:rm_cleanup_file_private()), and a UVM MM file holds the VA space file
it was initialized with (uvm.c:uvm_api_mm_initialize(), uvm_mm_release()).
NV00F8_CTRL_CMD_ATTACH_MEM and NV00FD_CTRL_CMD_ATTACH_MEM need nothing: the
driver removes such an attachment when the memory is freed
(rs_client.c:clientFreeResource_IMPL() => _clientUnmapInterBackRefMappings()).
With a limit, NV_ESC_EXPORT_TO_DMABUF_FD (GPUDirect RDMA, only with the rdma
driver capability) fails with NV_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED, since its dma-buf keeps
duplicates while the kernel holds it (nv-dmabuf.c), which nvproxy cannot see.
NV_MEMORY_EXPORT duplicates memory only in NV00E0_CTRL_CMD_EXPORT_MEM, which
nvproxy does not support.

The limit and the charges are saved with the sandbox. A restored sandbox keeps
the limit it was saved with, since nvproxy.Register is not called on restore
and the flag given to the restore is ignored, and its charges move to the
device instances that its devices are remapped to. nvproxy does not recreate
exports and UVM mappings on restore; their charges remain until their files
are closed. As for any object nvproxy cannot restore, a checkpoint fails while
a handle holds a charge that NV_ESC_RM_ALLOC did not make: an allocation by
NV_ESC_RM_VID_HEAP_CONTROL, or a duplicate or an import of charged memory.
Only the import is new, since nvproxy did not track imports before.

Not counted: memory the driver allocates for itself (channels, context
buffers, page tables), which grows with the number of contexts, channels and
mappings the application creates; and managed memory, whose pages UVM
allocates on fault and evicts when the GPU is full. Overcounted until their
range is freed or the VA space destroyed: UVM mappings that UVM also removes
in UVM_DISABLE_PEER_ACCESS, or in UVM_UNREGISTER_GPU of the GPU that owns
memory mapped on another GPU. Not modelled: on GPUs whose memory is onlined as
a NUMA node (Grace Hopper), PMA takes contiguous allocations from the kernel
in power-of-two blocks (_vidmemPmaAllocate()), which can exceed the rounded
size. Driver sources cited are open-gpu-kernel-modules 570.195.03.

Why: Orbit admits a sandbox to a GPU that other jobs share when the GPU has the
memory its Task declares free. Without a limit the sandbox can take the
others' memory and make their allocations fail; with it, the sandbox's own
allocation fails instead, as an ordinary out-of-memory error, and the sandbox
goes on.

Verified:
- gVisor's unit tests in its builder container, also under the race
  detector (--config=race): nvproxy_test's vidmem tests (the footprint;
  charging and refusal on several GPUs; fixed-address allocations; an unknown
  GPU; a driver that fails an allocation; NV_ESC_RM_VID_HEAP_CONTROL; an
  allocation the driver rounded beyond the limit, freed and refused, and kept
  when the free fails; duplicates; release by free, by a freed parent and by
  client teardown; a reused handle, also of a device with memory under it;
  exports to numbered slots holding memory until closed or overwritten, and
  imports holding it after; fabric objects, semaphore surfaces and memory
  mappers; the client locked while UVM maps memory; UVM mappings split,
  unmapped, replaced, freed with their range, removed with their GPU and
  released with their VA space, also by a racing close; a registered file and
  a UVM MM file holding the other file; the serialization of the export and
  import controls; remapping on restore; concurrent charges), and
  config_test's TestOverrideNVProxyGPUMemoryLimit (an annotation may set,
  match or lower the limit, not raise or remove it without
  --allow-flag-override). Breaking any one of these rules in the code, one at
  a time, fails a test, or for a racing close a race report. The ioctl
  handlers' copying of parameters from and to the application, and the
  dma-buf refusal, need a task and are not unit-tested.
- On an H100 80GB (driver 570.195.03, CUDA 12.8, rootless Docker 29.9.3,
  kernel 5.15), with a limit of 2 GiB set by annotation:
  - A CUDA context took 516 MiB of the limit (541065216 bytes in 16
    allocations; 608 MiB under PyTorch 2.7.1). nvproxy's total equalled
    NVML's used_memory for the Sentry at every check; the GPU's memory.used
    was 6 MiB higher per context.
  - cuMemAlloc beyond the limit returned CUDA_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY, cudaMalloc
    cudaErrorMemoryAllocation, and PyTorch raised torch.OutOfMemoryError, with
    and without expandable_segments, which filled the limit to the byte. Each
    process went on using the memory it had, later allocations within the
    limit succeeded, and the container kept running. A process whose context
    did not fit failed cuCtxCreate with CUDA_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY.
  - Allocations of odd sizes, from 1 byte to 3 MiB + 1, filled the limit
    exactly and never took NVML's used_memory for the Sentry past 2048 MiB.
    Under limits that are not a multiple of 2 MiB, allocations that the
    driver rounded beyond the limit were freed and refused, and every free
    succeeded.
  - Freeing, exit without freeing, SIGKILL and docker rm -f returned the
    memory, and repeated fill and free cycles ended at the same totals. Two
    processes in one sandbox shared the limit. Memory stayed charged while a
    cuMemMap mapping held it, also with its handle released while mapped,
    until cuMemUnmap; while an exported file descriptor held it, until the
    last process holding the descriptor closed it; while an import held it;
    and while a handle from cuIpcOpenMemHandle held it after the owner freed
    it. Every CUDA process registered its device files with
    NV_ESC_REGISTER_FD and initialized a UVM MM file, so both file holds were
    taken in every test.
  - An annotation of 1 GiB lowered a runtime limit of 2 GiB; 4 GiB and 0
    were refused at create. Without a limit, 6 GiB was allocated as before
    and nvproxy logged no charge.
  - cuMemGetInfo, torch.cuda.mem_get_info, NVML and nvidia-smi in the
    container reported the whole GPU, as under runc.
  CUDA 12.8 and PyTorch 2.7 used neither NV_ESC_RM_VID_HEAP_CONTROL nor
  fixed-address allocations, and freed a VMM allocation's handle only at
  cuMemUnmap, so the UVM-mapping holder was not reached on the GPU. Neither
  were fabric objects, semaphore surfaces, memory mappers, the dma-buf
  refusal, nor checkpoint and restore.

